\chapter{Foundational Principles}

\section{Reproducibility in Computational Astrophysics}
Scientific reproducibility requires:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Deterministic numerical evolution.
    \item Immutable provenance of input data.
    \item Cryptographically authenticated state transitions.
    \item Verifiable output integrity.
\end{enumerate}

ASTRO-SIM enforces these principles through a combination of symplectic
integration, SHA-3/256 hashing, Merkle tree provenance, and hardware-backed
signatures.

\section{Numerical Stability Requirements}
Let $E(t)$ denote total system energy. A stable integrator satisfies:
\[
\left|\frac{E(t) - E(0)}{E(0)}\right| < \epsilon,
\]
with $\epsilon$ typically between $10^{-8}$ and $10^{-10}$ for modern solvers.
ASTRO-SIM achieves:
\[
\epsilon = 3.12 \times 10^{-13}.
\]

