\chapter{System Architecture}

\section{Module A: Secure Data Ingestion}
Raw astrophysical datasets are ingested via zero-copy io\_uring buffers.
Each block $b_i$ is hashed:
\[
h_i = \text{SHA3-256}(b_i),
\]
forming the base layer of the provenance chain.

\section{Module B: Cryptographic Provenance Layer}
State transitions are authenticated using a Merkle tree:
\[
H_{\text{root}} = H(H_1 \parallel H_2 \parallel \cdots).
\]
Collision probability:
\[
\Pr(H' = H) = 2^{-256}.
\]

\section{Module C: Core Computational Kernels}
Physics modules include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Symplectic N-body solver.
    \item Barnes--Hut gravitational tree walk.
    \item AMR MHD solver.
    \item General relativistic ray-tracing.
\end{itemize}

\section{Module D: Distributed Execution Topology}
Execution occurs across GPU/CPU hybrid nodes connected via RDMA. Checkpoints
are stored on NVMe tiers with recovery time:
\[
t_{\text{rec}} = \frac{S}{B}.
\]

\section{Module E: Verification and Audit}
Final outputs are validated against the original hash ledger.

